---

import Image from "astro/components/Image.astro";
import Layout from "../Layout/Layout.astro";
import prison from "../assets/prison.webp";
import InLine from "../Components/InLine.astro";
import Record from "../assets/Prison_record.png";

const citations = [
    {
        Inline: "1",
        mla: "Paul Bowers. “A Trans South Carolinian Fights for Fair Treatment behind Bars - ACLU of South Carolina.” ACLU of South Carolina, 16 July 2024, www.aclusc.org/news/trans-south-carolinian-fights-fair-treatment-behind-bars/.",
    },
    {
        Inline: "2",
        mla: "Laird, Skylar. “Appeals Court Turns down ACLU’s Request to Interview SC Inmates • SC Daily Gazette.” SC Daily Gazette, 16 Dec. 2024, scdailygazette.com/2024/12/16/appeals-court-turns-down-aclus-request-to-interview-sc-inmates/.",
    },
    {
        Inline: "3",
        mla: "Collins, Jeffrey. “A Federal Judge Tosses a Lawsuit over the Ban on Recorded Inmate Interviews in South Carolina.” AP News, 9 Sept. 2024, apnews.com/article/aclu-free-speech-inmate-ban-interviews-29014db31f723c08f7a7b887b49fe8cb. Accessed 11 Feb. 2026.",
    },
    {
      Inline: "4",
      mla: "https://public.doc.state.sc.us/scdc-public/inmateDetails.do?id=%2000381710"
    }
]

---

<Layout title="Story" Citations={citations}>
    <div class="u-container flex flex-col content-center">
        <div class="flex-auto h-full w-full pt-30 z-2 text-center">
            <h1 class="text-4xl font-black py-5" data-sy-reveal="words">
                Sofia Cano's fight for healthcare.
            </h1>




            <span class="text-xl font-bold pl-10" data-sy-reveal="lines">5 years behind bars, 18 more to go, 6 years waiting.<InLine Citation={citations[0]} /></span>
            <div class="content-center justify-center">
                <span class="float-right">
                    <InLine Citation={citations[2]} />
                </span>
                <Image src={prison} alt="inline image" class="float-right h-70 w-96 object-contain right-30 inset-30 px-4"/>
                <p class="text-lg px-8 py-4">
                    She’s been in prison for 5 years now, and immensely regrets how she got into prison. Nevertheless, she makes the best of it. She’s currently in a college course for a bachelor’s in sociology. She runs a pathfinder game with some other inmates. Sofia Cano realized who she was when she was 16, but it took her 2 full years before she felt comfortable(or desperate) enough to talk to someone else about it. She legally changed her name, and has been diagnosed with gender dysphoria. Despite this, the prison she stays in as well as South Carolina denies she can start hormone replacement therapy.<InLine Citation={citations[1]}/> Such therapy would greatly relieve dysphoria which she feels, and help her become more comfortable with herself. The prison has been instructed to continue giving HRT to anyone that was taking it prior to being incarcerated, but refuses to allow Cano to begin it.
                </p>

                <p class="text-lg px-8 py-4">
                    For a time, Cano was provided razors to shave, as well as nail polish and makeup to allow some respite from dysphoria. That was until recently, when the warden of the prison decided that they should be taken away. The reason? The makeup could be used to hide cuts or bruises from officers to hide fights. Alternatively, they claimed it was possible they could be used to dress up pillows as people in escape attempts. It should be noted that Cano has no record of disciplinary sanctions or escape attempts, and currently works as a wardkeeper.<InLine Citation={citations[3]}/>
                </p>
                <div class="content-center justify-center grid w-full place-items-center w-2/4">
                    <Image src={Record} alt="A photo of Cano's prison record" class="w-2/4 object-contain"/>
                    <p class="pb-4 text-sm">Cano's disciplinary sanctions</p>
                </div>

                <p class="text-lg px-8 py-4" >
                    The South Carolina prison system has no reason to be denying Sofia Cano this treatment. The legal battle they are now fighting is undoubtedly costing them more than the treatment would have. Additionally, the treatment has no effect on the imprisonment of Cano. It’s important to note that she’s not asking for special treatment, just to be treated the same as those who transitioned prior to entering the prison.<InLine Citation={citations[1]}/> HRT wouldn’t change anything within the imprisonment process, and would only allow Cano to feel more comfortable in her body.
                </p>

                <p class="text-lg px-8 py-4" >
                    Now, Sofia Cano is in a legal battle with the prison for denying her access to HRT, as well as failing to change her name within the documents for her incarceration. She additionally is in the middle of a second battle over the denial of the ACLU being able to interview her about her experiences. The ACLU is representing her in both cases, and believes the lack of healthcare access to be unconstitutional, therefore bypassing the local laws. Sofia hopes to be able to win herself access to the care that she needs.
                </p>

                <p class="text-lg px-8 py-4" >
                    After being diagnosed with gender dysphoria, Cano legally changed her name. Despite this, all prison records reflect her deadname. Not only is this legally incorrect, it’s extremely disrespectful to Cano, and only makes it harder for her to live as herself.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</Layout>